			<td class="lt-chem-46734">
			<p class="lt-chem-46734"><b>Solution</b></p>

			<p class="lt-chem-46734">The molecular weight of compound <i>w</i> would be 31.0 g mole<sup>-1</sup>, and the percent composition would indicate 12.0 g or 1 mole of C per mole, 2.9 g or 3 moles of H, and only 16.0 g of sulfur. Hence the revised atomic weight of S would be 16.0 g mole<sup>-1</sup> and the formula for molecule <i>w</i> would be CH<sub>3</sub>S. The formula for <i>x</i> then would be CS<sub>4</sub>; for <i>y</i>, CH<sub>4</sub>S<sub>2</sub> ; and for <i>z</i>, C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub>S<sub>2</sub>. Needless to say, a compound such as <i>w</i> has never been observed, and the atomic weight of 32.0 for sulfur is valid.</p>
